'''Wellington Central''' is a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Wellington Central is [[Tamatha Paul]] of the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]]. She has held this position since the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Wellington Central – Preliminary Count |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electorate-details-60.html |access-date=23 October 2023 |website=Electoral Commission}}</ref>

The electorate will be abolished ahead of the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
From 1890 until 1905, the four main centres of New Zealand (Auckland, Wellington, Christchurch and Dunedin) were represented by three-member electorates. The City Single Electorates Act 1903 divided those electorates, effective from the end of the [[15th New Zealand Parliament|15th Parliament]]. The City of Wellington electorate was split into the [[Wellington East (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington East]], Wellington Central, and [[Wellington North (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington North]] electorates.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=66ff}} Wellington Central was focused on the city's central business district and surrounding suburbs.

As of 2020, Wellington Central covered the central city and its immediate suburban periphery from [[Karori]] in the west to the summit of [[Mount Victoria (Wellington hill)|Mount Victoria]] in the east, and southwards to a boundary with the [[Rongotai (New Zealand electorate)|Rongotai electorate]] near [[Wellington Hospital, New Zealand|Wellington Hospital]]. The northern boundary with [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]] ran through [[Wilton, New Zealand|Wilton]] and between [[Thorndon, New Zealand|Thorndon]] and [[Wadestown, New Zealand|Wadestown]] (which was in Wellington Central until 2014). From 1996 until 1999, its boundaries extended further north to include the suburbs of [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]] and [[Khandallah]].

Wellington Central has one of the most affluent and well-educated constituencies in New Zealand. It is home to many government agencies, as well as to the [[New Zealand Parliament Buildings]] and to two universities.

==History==
Wellington Central was established in 1905 when the multi-member urban electorate [[Wellington (New Zealand electorate)|City of Wellington]] was replaced by three new seats: [[Wellington East (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington East]], [[Wellington North (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington North]] and Wellington Central. A prominent holder of the seat was [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] leader [[Peter Fraser]], who was [[Prime Minister of New Zealand|Prime Minister]] from 1940 to 1949. 

The electorate was abolished in 1993, when a redistribution moved its boundary west, replaced by [[Wellington-Karori]]. Three years later, Wellington Central was recreated with a larger catchment as one of the 65 electorates used in the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 election]]. [[ACT New Zealand]] leader [[Richard Prebble]] won the electorate following [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] leader [[Jim Bolger]]'s endorsement of Prebble over National candidate Mark Thomas, in order for the new ACT party to get into parliament. This election was featured in the documentary film ''Campaign'' directed by [[Tony Sutorius]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nzonscreen.com/title/campaign|title=Campaign {{!}} Film {{!}} NZ On Screen|website=Nzonscreen.com|others=NZ On Screen|access-date=14 September 2017}}</ref>

Labour candidates [[Marian Hobbs]] and [[Grant Robertson]] held the seat from 1999 until 2023, when it was won by [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] candidate [[Tamatha Paul]].

The 2025 boundary review required a reduction in North Island electorates by one to reflect nationwide population shifts. The three Wellington City electorates ([[Rongotai (electorate)|Rongotai]], Wellington Central and [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]) were all underpopulated. All were abolished. The population of Wellington Central was redistributed to {{NZ electorate link|Wellington North}} and {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Bays}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=21 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

